# Caddyfile TEMPLATE — do not edit "Caddyfile" directly, it's generated
# from this file by deploy.sh every run and will be overwritten.
#
# The site address below is filled in with the VM's current public IP by
# deploy.sh.
#
# --- TLS status: DEFERRED ON PURPOSE (not an oversight) -----------------
# We tried serving this over HTTPS with "tls internal" (Caddy's own local
# CA issuing a self-signed cert for the bare IP, since no public domain is
# configured here). Certificate issuance itself worked, but every client
# (browser and curl, from both outside the VM and via loopback on the VM
# itself) failed the actual TLS handshake with a low-level
# "internal_error" alert from Caddy — reproduced across a fresh cert and a
# fully wiped certificate store, so it isn't cert corruption. Root cause
# not yet confirmed (candidate: a TLS 1.3 negotiation incompatibility).
# See the project plan doc for the full diagnostic trail.
#
# Given the project deadline, we deliberately paused that investigation
# and are serving plain HTTP for now via the explicit "http://" scheme
# below, which tells Caddy not to attempt automatic HTTPS for this site
# at all (so it can't hit that bug). Caddy is still doing its job as a
# reverse proxy in front of Nextcloud — just without TLS termination.
#
# --- To re-enable HTTPS later, if time allows ---------------------------
# 1. Change the site address below from "http://{{VM_PUBLIC_IP}}" to just
#    "{{VM_PUBLIC_IP}}" (drop the "http://" prefix).
# 2. Add a "tls internal" line back inside the block.
# 3. In deploy.sh: change wait_for_health()'s URL back to "https://" (with
#    curl's -k flag, since it's still a self-signed cert), and change
#    ensure_reverse_proxy_config()'s overwriteprotocol value back to
#    "https".
# 4. Re-run deploy.sh, then pick the TLS debugging back up from the
#    project plan doc's diagnostic trail (the loopback + --tls-max 1.2
#    test was the next planned step).

http://{{VM_PUBLIC_IP}} {
	reverse_proxy app:80
}
